# Line endings for the files that Linux executes rather than a human reads.
#
# This repository is written on Windows and runs on Ubuntu. What is committed is
# already LF, so a clone on Linux has always been fine. The problem is the
# Windows working copy: `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites these to CRLF on every
# checkout, and `bench/deploy/deploy.sh` copies to the host from the working
# copy, not from git. So whether the box receives LF or CRLF depends on whether
# git happened to re-materialise a file since it was last edited, which is not a
# thing anybody should be depending on.
#
# It matters because on Linux a carriage return is not cosmetic. The kernel
# reads the shebang literally, so `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` requests an
# interpreter named "bash\r" and fails with
#
#     ./deploy.sh: /usr/bin/env: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# naming the interpreter that does exist and saying nothing about the character
# that is actually wrong. Unit files fail more quietly: systemd keeps the
# trailing CR as part of the value.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf for these paths, so the working copy is LF on
# every platform and the transfer has nothing left to get wrong.
#
# Scoped rather than blanket on purpose. A repository-wide `* text=auto eol=lf`
# renormalises every tracked file at once; tried here, it rewrote two unrelated
# CSV manifests and buried the change that mattered in four thousand lines.

*.sh      text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
